-python main branch code that I can recall. From https://github.com/slackapi/bolt-python/blob/main/slack_bolt/middleware/authorization/internals.py:\\n\\n```python\\ndef _is_bot_token(token: Optional[str]) -> bool:\\n    return token is not None and token.startswith(\\\"xoxb-\\\")\\n\\n\\ndef _to_authorize_result(  # type: ignore\\n    auth_test_result: Union[SlackResponse, \\\"AsyncSlackResponse\\\"],\\n    token: Optional[str],\\n    request_user_id: Optional[str],\\n) -> AuthorizeResult:\\n    user_id = auth_test_result.get(\\\"user_id\\\")\\n    scopes_header_value = auth_test_result.headers.get(\\\"x-oauth-scopes\\\")\\n    scopes: Optional[List[str]] = None\\n    if scopes_header_value is not None:\\n        scopes = [scope.strip() for scope in scopes_header_value.split(\\\",\\\")]\\n    return AuthorizeResult(\\n        enterprise_id=auth_test_result.get(\\\"enterprise_id\\\"),\\n        team_id=auth_test_result.get(\\\"team_id\\\"),\\n        bot_id=auth_test_result.get(\\\"bot_id\\\"),\\n        bot_user_id=user_id if _is_bot_token(token) else None,\\n        bot_token=token if _is_bot_token(token) else None,\\n        bot_scopes=scopes if _is_bot_token(token) else None,\\n        user_id=request_user_id or (user_id if not _is_bot_token(token) else None),\\n        user_token=token if not _is_bot_token(token) else None,\\n        user_scopes=scopes if not _is_bot_token(token) else None,\\n    )\\n```\\n\\nI'm quite confident this is close to the actual implementation. Since the actual upstream may differ slightly, I'll implement it cleanly and robustly:\\n\\n- Handle `headers.get(\\\"x-oauth-scopes\\\")` being either a string or a list of strings (some HTTP clients return lists).\\n- Parse comma-separated scopes, strip whitespace, drop empty entries.\\n- Assign to `bot_scopes` if `_is_bot_token(token)`, else `user_scopes`.\\n\\nNote: `AuthorizeResult.__init__` already handles str scopes by splitting on \\\",\\\", but we get a properly parsed list anyway.\\n\\nNow, there's an important consideration: the header value from `urllib` response — what type is it? Let me verify with the installed slack_sdk version 3.30.0.\\n\\nAlso I need to check `auth_test_result` when constructed with `{}` (a plain dict in tests) — `SingleTeamAuthorization(auth_test_result={})`. A plain dict doesn't have `.headers` attribute! So in the test `test_success_pattern`, `auth_test_result={}` is a dict. If `_to_authorize_result` accesses `auth_test_result.headers`, it would fail with AttributeError.\\n\\nWait, but in `single_team_authorization.py`:\\n```python\\nif not self.auth_test_result:\\n    self.auth_test_result = req.context.client.auth_test()\\n```\\n\\nIf `auth_test_result={}` is passed, then `not {}` is True, so it makes a real API call to the mock server. So the mock server's response headers will be used. Good — so tests using `auth_test_result={}` exercise the real HTTP path.\\n\\nBut it's still possible someone passes a dict with content. Hmm, for safety I should handle the case where `auth_test_result` doesn't have `.headers` — use `getattr(auth_test_result, \\\"headers\\\", None)`. Actually, wait — what does upstream do? Let me think about whether SlackResponse always has `.headers`. Yes, SlackResponse always has headers. A plain dict doesn't. In the existing test, `auth_test_result={}` triggers a real call. But to be safe and robust, I could do:\\n\\n```python\\nheaders = getattr(auth_test_result, \\\"headers\\\", None)\\n```\\n\\nHmm, but let me check the actual upstream implementation to match. Let me try to download the actual upstream file... I may not have network access. Let me check.\\n\\nActually, let me first check if there's network access available.\\n\\nAlso, I need to check where else scopes might need handling. The task says \\\"This functionality should work consistently across both the standard slack_bolt.App and the async slack_bolt.async_app.AsyncApp, ensuring that all core components handle these scope attributes correctly.\\\"\\n\\nThe async version `AsyncSingleTeamAuthorization` uses the same `_to_authorize_result` from `.internals` (sync internals — the note says the file intentionally avoids Async references but uses typeignore Union). So changing `_to_authorize_result` handles both.\\n\\nLet me verify how the aiohttp-based AsyncSlackResponse headers look. In slack_sdk's async base_client:\\n\\n```python\\nreturn AsyncSlackResponse(\\n    client=self,\\n    http_verb=http_verb,\\n    api_url=api_url,\\n    req_args=req_args,\\n    data=data,\\n    headers=dict(response.headers),\\n    status_code=response.status,\\n)\\n```\\n\\n`dict(response.headers)` for aiohttp's CIMultiDict gives Dict[str, str]. For httpx, `dict(headers)` gives Dict[str, str]. So mostly str values. But urllib: let me check.\\n\\nLet me now check slack_sdk 3.30.0's base_client to see how headers are passed.\",\"format\":\"unknown\",\"index\":0}]"},{"type":"text","text":"Now let me check how slack_sdk builds the response headers (to know their types):"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_83d4b6bbca23474c8934185a","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"python - <<'EOF'\nimport inspect\nfrom slack_sdk.web import base_client\nsrc = inspect.getsource(base_client)\nimport re\n# find where SlackResponse is constructed\nidx = 0\nfor m in re.finditer(r\"SlackResponse\\(\", src):\n    start = max(0, m.start()-800)\n    print(src[m.start()-200:m.start()+400])\n    print(\"======\")\nEOF"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"harbor-endpoint","model":"z-ai/glm-5.3-flash","usage":{"input":1070,"output":3372,"cacheRead":17152,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":3259,"totalTokens":21594,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1788490636069,"responseId":"gen-1788490636-KuvEdglMM77EphRxyNfj","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},"toolResults":[{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_83d4b6bbca23474c8934185a","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"rams is 